/** Editorial translations for siman 197 failing blocks — tevilah on Shabbat */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '2#ב': `Birth. Specifically when she immerses after childbirth and did not see impure blood — that is, after thirty-three days for a male and sixty-six for a female. But if she saw impure blood and her time of immersion falls on Friday night, she is permitted to immerse, for it is at her proper time — so Mahari"v in his rulings. And regarding when her husband was not in the city, Bach wrote that it is forbidden to immerse on Shabbat only when it was possible for her to immerse before Shabbat and her husband was in the city; but if her husband was not in the city and he comes on Friday evening, even though it was possible for her to immerse beforehand (since she did not neglect by failing to immerse earlier), she is permitted to immerse on Shabbat — and so Shach. But Taz ruled that there is no distinction at all between whether her husband was in the city or not and only came on Friday evening; everything depends on whether she was able to immerse beforehand without any oness — then she is forbidden to immerse on Shabbat, even if her husband did not come until Friday evening (and in Shulchan Aruch he elaborated on this and upheld his words in Shach).`,
    '2#ד': `First. Because he acquires her through this intercourse, as written in Orach Chayyim siman 39 and Even HaEzer siman 63 — therefore one should not marry a widow on Shabbat when her immersion falls on Friday night. And if she immersed before Shabbat, he must come upon her specifically on Friday evening after the chuppah; yichud in one room does not help so that he may have first intercourse with her on Shabbat. So too Mahari"l — we require complete intercourse specifically. Even though in Sefer Masat Binyamin siman 90 it is written that yichud also acquires in a place fit for intercourse, nevertheless, since many great ones forbid, one should be stringent end quote Taz.`,
    '3#ב': `Her daughter. And even if she has no daughter — the law is the same, for we do not distinguish. And in a responsum, Maharam Lublin was asked about a woman who did not immerse properly in a case where after two or three days she needs to return and immerse — whether she may immerse by day, and whether she needs to guard zavah days because of sarach batah, lest she say she saw blood and immersed after three days — and Maharam did not answer on this. It appears that regarding the first question one should be stringent lechatchila, and bediavad it is permitted; and regarding the second question the matter is simple that she may immerse after two or three days, and we do not find a decree of sarach batah except regarding immersion by day. See siman 196 seif 12 end quote Shach. And Bach wrote that even to go from her house to the mikveh voluntarily is forbidden — namely, when the woman bathes and combs at home and goes to the place of immersion. But when there is a bathhouse and mikveh in one place, and a woman goes voluntarily to the bathhouse an hour or two before dark and comes home after dark — even though the bathhouse and mikveh are close to her home, there is no sarach batah here, for the daughter knows that she lingers bathing and combing in the bathhouse and does not immerse until after dark end quote. However, in Agur it is written that the Ashkenaz custom is to immerse near dark; and Shach wrote that this means she comes home after it grows dark — and it seems to me that so they practice. Nevertheless, one should be stringent. However, it appears that this is specifically on day eight, where immersion is forbidden only because of sarach batah alone — in such a case they are lenient near dark. But on day seven one should not immerse near dark at all, and one should protest against those who do so.`,
    '3#ג': `And kalot. Shach wrote: it appears that this is specifically for us, where kalot do not immerse on the seventh day — as is clear in Mahari"l, who deals with such a case. But in a place where kalot immerse on the seventh day, or for us if she had a mishap during her counting days and then immerses immediately after seven — she cannot immerse by day, for on day seven it is forbidden not only because of sarach batah alone, but lest she see and annul retroactively and be found a zavah retroactively end quote. And in truth his words are astonishing, and it is uncertain — for the concern lest she annul is in this manner: that the Sages were concerned lest she have relations while it is still day, and afterward see again on that same day and annul retroactively, and it be found that he came upon her in prohibition. And this is specifically regarding an ordinary woman. But regarding a kalah, per what Rama wrote — the reason that they do not come to the chassan until the night — this concern does not apply to her at all. Uncertain; examine.`,
    '5#_': `She transgressed. And Bach ruled to be stringent like Mahari"l, that on day seven her immersion did not count and she needs a second immersion at night; and it is good to be stringent where possible, since so too is the view of Raavad and other great poskim end quote Shach (and in Hagahot Ish he wrote: therefore those women whose immersion fell on day seven, Friday evening, and they immersed before dark — their immersion did not count and they need a second immersion on Friday night. And so I heard in the name of the Gaon Rabbi Yehoshua Av Beit Din of Krakow, who ruled halachah lemaaseh and required a second immersion. And so Bava Kamma page 117 — that they act improperly by immersing on Friday night while it is still day. And so in a responsum of Even HaEzer, question twenty, Friday evening).`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#א': `Language of Rambam chapter 4 of Issurei Biah, from the baraita in Shabbat page 64 in the name of R. Akiva, and Rav Hai Gaon explained: we learned immersion for a niddah from the Torah by kal vachomer from her contact, as it is said, "and one who touches her bed shall wash in water." And Rashi explains: we learned from a middah vessel, as it is said there, "only in niddah water shall he cleanse," and we expound at the end of Avodah Zarah, "in water that a niddah immerses in," etc. Tosafot Yevamot page 47 side b, and the Rosh wrote them at the end of Niddah.`,
    '2#_': `Beit Yosef, per the view of Rashi that the Tur brought — that tevilah at its proper time is not a mitzvah except when her husband is in the city, as Tosafot wrote in Niddah page 36 side a and Yoma page 8 side a. And from Yehoshua we find that he was punished for preventing Israel from being fruitful and multiplying for one night. And he wrote further: and if she delays immersing in order to distress her husband, it is a sin in her hands. And in the name of Sefer HaZohar — a woman who does so causes many evils in the world. See there.`,
    '5#ב': `There, in the name of Rashba in the name of R. Eliezer, and Rashba agreed; and so too Ramban. See Beit Yosef.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `Unless they immersed properly. As written in Yevamot page 47 side b, "and everything that interposes," etc., and Rashi there, s.v. chotzetz, etc.`,
    '2#א': `If, etc. In the Gemara page 15 side b: a woman who established a veset for her husband, etc., and it says in the Yerushalmi on this statement that it is forbidden for a woman to stand in her tumah — but he explains per the one who says tevilah at its proper time is a mitzvah, for the language does not imply so. And Avodah HaGolah. See Zohar in Saba.`,
    '2#ג': `But otherwise it is forbidden. For we hold per the one who says tevilah at its proper time is not a mitzvah — and Tosafot in Niddah page 30 side a, s.v. vesham, etc., and Yoma page 8 side a, s.v. dechol, etc. But at the end of Yoma it implies that even per that view, nevertheless it is permitted even on Yom Kippur. And Tosafot in Shabbat page 121 side a, s.v. hag, etc. — all the more so on Shabbat, as written in Yoma page 18 side b: satisfactory on Shabbat, etc. But Tosafot there, s.v. kol, wrote that this is specifically in their days for taharot, etc. Nevertheless they only wrote about Yom Kippur, like Rav.`,
    '2#ד': `And if it was, etc. But in the Gemara in Yoma there it implies even if possible — for he distinguishes in the mishnah between a person and vessels, and regarding vessels on Yom Tov they immerse voluntarily, as stated in the Gemara (page 18 side a): a vessel that became tamei from Yom Tov, etc. — unlike what Darkei Moshe wrote. And this is what he says here; but in a place, etc.`,
    '2#ה': `And in a place where they practiced, etc. From what is stated in siman 68 seif 1, Lek — this, that it is possible, etc. (And likewise a widow, etc., because it is forbidden, etc. See our Rabbi in Even HaEzer siman 64 s.k. 11).`,
    '5#ב': `And nevertheless not, etc. Yes, this is explained in Beit Yosef as the words of Agur; but the main point is specifically on the seventh, Friday evening (and see Bach).`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 197 in Taz s.k. 4) He was astonished at Rama and those who follow his view; and in Siftei Kohen s.k. 3 I upheld Rama's words, only that I wrote briefly. And now I am compelled to elaborate and copy what I said in my book Vayivchanu — examine my words. And this is my language: Bach Chadash wrote that one should be stringent, for this stringency is from the law according to the plain meaning in Mordechai, per the view of Mahari"v that tevilah at its proper time is a mitzvah — see there. And per my humble view it appears that one should not be stringent, as I will explain. And it appears to me that the main point is that we hold tevilah at its proper time is not a mitzvah, as Tosafot wrote in chapter HaMapeles (Niddah page 30) and in the first chapter of Yoma page 8 side a: ma'asim b'chol yom — you have no woman who immerses at her proper time, and not at the time of immersion she keeps day against day for zavah. And in the last chapter of Niddah we also say: since nowadays we made them all doubt zavot, we immerse them on the daytime of the seventh — implying that they never immersed by day at all until here is his language. And what Bach Chadash said on this — per my humble view this is not a difficulty, for since nowadays, after R. Zeira's stringency, this is her time — after she sees a drop of blood like mustard she counts seven clean days; if so, now it is incumbent upon her to immerse immediately, and if she does not immerse immediately after her count she transgresses the mitzvah until here is his language. He did not intend well, for certainly Tosafot do not prove from what they immerse after counting seven clean days, but from what they do not immerse at their proper time — namely, keeping day against day for zavah and niddah — and on this one should not answer anything at all, for certainly because of R. Zeira's stringency she will not transgress the mitzvah by not immersing at her proper time. And further, it is possible for her to fulfill both. And so ma'asim b'chol yom — you have no woman who immerses at her proper time when her husband is not in the city; and so widows and virgins who are niddot do not immerse at all at their proper time. And so in all these countries, where even if she did not have relations she does not immerse until after five and seven days, as explained in the previous siman seif 11 — and this is an extra stringency — if so, how would she transgress the mitzvah by not immersing at her proper time? From all this it appears that we hold tevilah at its proper time is not a mitzvah. See there. Nevertheless, even so it is permitted to immerse on Shabbat, as Terumas HaDeshen wrote siman 255, and Beit Yosef — for from Beitzah page 18 it is proven that even when not at her proper time it is permitted to immerse on Shabbat, for there is no prohibition in the matter because it appears like heating water (miykar). And certainly so it is proven there, for Beit Shammai hold that a person and vessels immerse on erev Shabbat — obviously even erev Shabbat is their time, and even so Beit Hillel say regarding a person on Shabbat. And so it is proven per my view in Tur Orach Chayyim, that in siman 327 he wrote a tamei person is permitted to immerse on Shabbat from his tumah because it appears like miykar; and in siman 613 he wrote similarly, that all those obligated in immersions immerse in their usual manner on Yom Kippur. And Rav Tam wrote that one does not immerse — and the baraita "they immerse" means specifically according to the one who says tevilah at its proper time is a mitzvah; but we hold it is not a mitzvah, therefore let him wait until tomorrow end quote. And in chapter 2 of Beitzah we say: satisfactory on Shabbat, on Yom Kippur (meaning that miykar is not relevant, for bathing is forbidden on Yom Kippur) — what is there to say? Rava said: is there anything permitted on Shabbat (and it does not appear like fixing a person, because it appears like miykar), and on Yom Kippur it is forbidden? Rather, since on Shabbat it is permitted, on Yom Kippur too it is permitted (and see Bach siman 554 end of seif 3). But certainly it is clear to the Tur that on Yom Kippur, since there is also a prohibition of bathing, it is not permitted except for the reason that tevilah at its proper time is a mitzvah; however, for this reason alone it would not be permitted, because in any case it appears like fixing a person. And so Tosafot wrote in Shabbat page 111 side a, and these are their words: and if you say, let us establish this baraita "they immerse in their usual manner" according to the one who says tevilah at its proper time is a mitzvah, and Rav according to the one who says it is not a mitzvah — one may say that because of tevilah at its proper time being a mitzvah alone, the prohibition of fixing a person would not be permitted end quote. But since on Shabbat it is permitted because it appears like miykar, on Yom Kippur too it is permitted. However, on Shabbat it is permitted even when not at her proper time; but on Yom Kippur it is not permitted except because of mitzvah. And so it is proven in Tosafot in Beitzah there — for why do we say there "since," etc. — we challenge: perhaps it is not so, that only on Shabbat it is permitted and not on Yom Kippur? And we answer: it cannot enter your mind, for we taught "all those obligated in immersions immerse in their usual manner." And Tosafot wrote, and these are their words: "all those obligated in immersions immerse in their usual manner" means specifically for those who hold tevilah at its proper time is a mitzvah; but now, since all our women's immersions are not at their proper time, they do not immerse on Yom Kippur until here is their language. And at first glance there is a difficulty in their words, for the mishnah of Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel deals with immersion not at its proper time. And further, if so, why does Rava need a reason to distinguish between a person and vessels — because a person appears like miykar? Let it emerge from the fact that regarding a person tevilah at its proper time is a mitzvah. And further, in chapter HaMapeles and Yoma Tosafot wrote that Rabbi Yossi bar Rabbi Yehuda holds that both Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel hold tevilah at its proper time is not a mitzvah — if so, there is a difficulty from the mishnah in Beitzah, and it is forced to say that he also argues on that mishnah; if so, we should not rule like him. Rather, it is certainly simple that on Shabbat, even when not at her proper time and even when it is not a mitzvah, it is permitted; but on Yom Kippur it is not permitted except because of mitzvah. And now the difficulty is resolved, that Tosafot wrote so regarding immersion on Yom Kippur, and did not write so regarding the mishnah of Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel regarding immersion on Shabbat — but certainly as I separated. And so it is proven from the words of Semag, lavin 65, page 22 end side b, and Rashba, who wrote anonymously in the laws of Shabbat that a tamei person is permitted to immerse on Shabbat — implying even when not at his proper time. But Mordechai wrote that Rashi ruled that tevilah at its proper time is not a mitzvah, and from this he forbids immersing on Shabbat (and Mahari"v followed him), and he is alone against all the poskim, and the judgment does not follow the halachah — and not like what Darkei Moshe wrote, that one may say to Mordechai that what we say in Beitzah, "a tamei person is permitted to immerse on Shabbat," means specifically according to the one who says tevilah at its proper time is a mitzvah, etc. — and this is certainly not so. See there, and further it is proven that Tosafot wrote at the end of Niddah, and the Rosh there, and Semag lavin 111 page 38 side b, and Semak, and Agudah, and other poskim in the name of Rashi, that one should not comb on Friday evening and immerse on Shabbat, because it is two leniencies end quote. And it is explained below siman 198 seif 7, explicitly, that it is permitted to immerse on Shabbat according to Rashi, even though Bach wrote that Rashi wrote so only according to the words of Rabbi Meshulam, and he himself does not hold so — this is not acceptable to the mind. And it is more reasonable to say, as Terumas HaDeshen wrote, that Mordechai in the name of Rashi also deals only when her husband is not in the city end quote. And further, in chapter Kol Kitvei (Shabbat page 121) Tosafot wrote that the version of Rashi and Rashi is "one need not immerse," etc. — that even according to the one who says tevilah at its proper time is not a mitzvah, it is permitted to immerse, as implied at the end of tractate Yoma, etc. See there. If so, it is clear to Rashi that even when it is not a mitzvah one may immerse, even on Yom Kippur; and so Tosafot conclude in Beitzah there, and in chapter Shmonah Sheratzim (Shabbat page 111 side a). And it is certainly proven from the whole sugya at the end of Yoma. See there; examine. General principle: it emerges that even though tevilah at its proper time is not a mitzvah, it is permitted to immerse on Shabbat. But Terumas HaDeshen wrote that nowadays they practice to be stringent regarding a yoledet and the like, and he forced a reason, writing and these are his words: since the custom now is to be careful and prevent heating on Shabbat, even though there is no reason in the matter — nevertheless one cannot say that it appears like miykar, for they are careful about heating, and it is proven that their intent is to immerse, and it appears like fixing. And for this reason women practice to be careful not to immerse on Shabbat unless her husband is in the city and it was not possible for her beforehand, for in such a case it is a mitzvah because of onah end quote. It is implied from his words that when her husband is not in the city and he comes on Friday evening, even though it was possible beforehand — nevertheless, since she did not neglect by failing to immerse earlier, it is permitted to immerse. And so Beit Yosef ruled that it is permitted to immerse on Shabbat in every case — and this is the main halachah, as I separated. Therefore, in a place where her husband is in the city one should not be stringent, as Rama wrote. And so too Maharam Lublin in responsum siman 53 and siman 71 — see there until here is my language in my book. Behold, it has been clarified that Rama's words here in the hagahah are clear and correct. The ordinances of Hashem are upright.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '2#_': `Friday night. See in responsum Chemdat Tzvi siman 11, who forbids women to immerse on Friday night in hot water, because it is forbidden to wash one's whole body in hot water on Shabbat, as written in Orach Chayyim siman 326. [And he wrote that one who transgresses and immerses on Friday night in hot water has certainly done a prohibition, and it is permitted to call her a transgressor, etc. And what one sage ruled and permitted them to immerse on Friday evening while it is still day on their seventh day — he certainly did not act well, and he transgressed a rabbinic prohibition, etc. And he elaborated on this and concluded, writing at the end: halachah lemaaseh I say that they should not immerse in hot water on Friday night, nor on Friday evening that is their seventh day; rather, let them immerse in cold water on Friday night, or only by cooling their heat until the name "hot water" is not called upon them; or let them postpone the immersion until Motzaei Shabbat. And if it is impossible in all that was said, let them permit them to immerse during bein hashemashot, and let them not come to their homes until night — and more than this we have no authority to permit at all end quote. See there. And see in Bach at the end of siman 190, who brought this and challenged "or postpone the immersion to Motzaei Shabbat" — for Rama wrote that in a place where they practiced to be stringent, also on Motzaei Shabbat she should not immerse, etc. See there. Also in Levushi Serad, in novel laws at the end of the book, he addressed this. And it is possible to reconcile it, for Rama concludes that since it was possible for her to immerse beforehand, etc. — and here it is called impossible, for she cannot bear the coldness of the water.] And see in responsum Divrei Yosef siman 64, who brought his words — and these are his words: he came to justify the custom that the daughters of Israel practiced, to immerse in hot water on Friday night; and at the end he left the matter uncertain. See there. And see in responsum Noda Biyhudah, second part, Orach Chayyim siman 24, and similarly that he would warn the bath attendants to put hot water on Friday evening into the mikveh while it is still day, so that when it grows dark they will be only lukewarm; and in lukewarm water it is permitted — only in hot water it is forbidden. See there. Further, regarding Yom Tov that falls on Friday evening — whether it is permitted to kindle under the pot for the need of a Jewish bather in the bathhouse, and the rest he leaves to drip into the mikveh for the need of women's immersion on Friday night. See there. And see in responsum Tiferet Tzvi, part Orach Chayyim siman 9, and in responsum Maor HaGolah, Rabbi Akiva Eiger siman 17, further on the aforementioned case. And see in responsum Chut HaShani siman 83, on the matter of women's immersion on Friday night — he hesitates to mention immersing while it is still day in a place where there is no custom; and in a place where they practice so, one should do so only on Shabbat after the recitation of Barchu, when the name Shabbat has already been called upon it; but not on a weekday, even after Barchu. See there. And see in responsum Chinuch Beit Yehudah siman 82, who challenged the custom that even on Shabbat after the recitation of Barchu it is not correct to immerse while it is still day, and one should protest and abolish this custom. And if she fears concern of a fall, let them stand a candle in a closed lantern in the mikveh building voluntarily; and even during bein hashemashot it is permitted to kindle via a gentile, for telling a gentile is shevut, and all shevut is permitted during bein hashemashot for the need of a mitzvah. And we are not concerned lest the candle tilt — for there are at least two women who will remind one another. See there.`,
    '4#_': `After childbirth. Ba'er Heitev wrote: specifically, etc., until that is, after thirty-three for a male and sixty-six for a female. And see in Dagul MeRevavah, who disagrees on this, that even within the days of tohar, if she saw and could not immerse only because of the stringency that the poskim were stringent — it is called her proper time, and she is permitted to immerse on Friday night. See there. And so in Sefer Levushi Serad siman 86. And he wrote further that if she already immersed once within the days of tohar after childbirth, and afterward saw within the days of tohar and her time of immersion arrived on Friday night — in this too Shach agrees that she is permitted to immerse; for either way, if we hold this blood to be tamei, it is an immersion of mitzvah; and if not, there is no prohibition of immersion at all. See there. And know that in all that I wrote above, siman 196 s.k. 13, that she can count seven clean days immediately — but she was stringent on herself and did not begin counting until after five days; and through this her night of immersion fell on Friday night — she is forbidden to immerse, and this is simple.`,
    '5#_': `That she is forbidden to immerse. See in responsum Chut HaShani siman 70, who wrote that bediavad, if she immersed — if unintentionally, she is permitted immediately; and if intentionally she immersed on Friday night, one should be stringent to forbid her to her husband on Shabbat; and in what he wrote "she is permitted" — and if it is Yom Tov, it appears halachically that she is permitted immediately; but not lemaaseh, for it is fitting to be stringent with one who acted intentionally, because of making a fence around the matter. See there. And it appears to me that in any case she is permitted in all kinds of closeness and affection [now I saw in Minchat Shlomo and S.T. that their view is to be lenient on Yom Tov even lechatchila, even in places where they practiced to be stringent on Shabbat; and regarding Shabbat, Minchat Shlomo's view is like the aforementioned Chut HaShani — that if she immersed intentionally she is forbidden for that Shabbat; and S.T. disagrees, etc.; and per my humble view it appears to be lenient bediavad, even on that same Shabbat, etc. See there. And so it is somewhat implied from a responsum of Chakham Tzvi that I brought above s.k. 1, in what he wrote "she did a prohibition," etc. See there.] And see further in responsum Chut HaShani siman 82.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 197 s.k. 3 in the hagahah) They may immerse by day. Where they practice to immerse a kalah on day seven, she should not immerse until after sunrise; and bediavad, from dawn it is effective, as stated in the mishnah at the beginning of chapter 2 of Megillah. And in Tur it is written: but a zavah from the Torah immerses on day seven after sunrise. This wording is somewhat difficult, for from the Torah, after dawn she is permitted to immerse.`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '2#א': `"If she could not, etc." And it is written in a responsum of Maharam Lublin siman 71: one who immersed on the night of the sixth and afterward found that she had not immersed properly — for example, she found dirt under the nail — she is permitted to immerse on Friday night, for it is an oness; and one should not compare her to a yoledet. And one who immersed on Shabbat, and when she came home from the mikveh she saw that she had forgotten to cut one nail, and there is no dirt under the nail — it appears that even though the need for a second immersion in such a case is a general stringency, as I wrote below siman 198 s.k. 25 — nevertheless she should return and immerse; and we do not say that it is an immersion that is not of mitzvah, for by law all immersion is permitted on Shabbat, as I wrote below shortly. And even though we practice to be stringent in such a case, they did not practice so; and further, since they already practiced to be stringent to require a second immersion for her — if so, it is an immersion of mitzvah. And further, Rav Hai holds that the nail impedes, and I brought him in siman 198 there. And further, below s.k. 25 it is explained that even immersion not at its proper time is permitted — only Terumas HaDeshen wrote that they practice to be stringent because in the Gemara it says that it does not appear like fixing a person, because it appears like miykar; and in this time, when they are careful about heating, it appears like fixing a person. Obviously a pure person is permitted to immerse, for it does not appear like fixing a person at all — and so it is simple in the Gemara and poskim; and Tosefta in Orach Chayyim siman 326, that a pure person is permitted to wash his whole body and immerse on Shabbat (and even one who forbids a niddah to immerse when not at her proper time agrees regarding a pure person, as is proven in the Gemara and all the poskim). If so, this woman is permitted to immerse — either way: if the nail impedes, then it is tevilah at its proper time for the need of mitzvah; and if it does not impede, then she is pure and one does not fix a woman at all, and she is permitted to immerse. So it appears to me clearly, not like one who wished to be stringent that she should not immerse until after Shabbat. And per this it appears to me that the same applies in every other safek whether she immersed properly or not — she is permitted to immerse on Friday night. And for this reason it appears to me regarding one who immersed on the night of the sixth, and the next day found that she had forgotten to cut one nail — that in the responsum of Maharam Lublin there he ruled that she needs no immersion at all, for they were not stringent in such a case in the law of forgetting a nail, since she passed one night with her husband. And below siman 198 s.k. 25 I wrote that even in such a case she needs another immersion — if so, the questioner's question there remains in its place. And these are its words: since by the law of the Gemara and the poskim she needs no other immersion for forgetting a nail — if so, it is not considered that she delayed her time, for she did all that was in her power to do, and the prohibition was not known to her until the morrow; or let us say all the more that she is forbidden to immerse — for if we permit her to immerse, it is a stringency that leads to a leniency, for that woman had a valid immersion by law, and if so she stands in the prohibition of bathing on Shabbat like any other person. But the later authorities were stringent upon her and required another immersion; and if we permit her immersion on Shabbat, it is a stringency that leads to a leniency, for we permit the prohibition of bathing on Shabbat end quote. And it appears to me that there is no stringency here that leads to a leniency; on the contrary, when there is no need for immersion — for the nail does not impede — if so she is pure, and there is no prohibition of bathing on Shabbat for a pure person, for it does not appear like fixing. And if so, this woman is permitted to immerse on Friday night either way — and this is clear; and so agreed the Gaon, my master and teacher, may his memory be blessed.`,
    '2#ג': `"But in a place where there is no custom, one should not be stringent, etc." And Bach wrote that one should be stringent, for this stringency is from the law, etc.; also tevilah at its proper time is a mitzvah. See there — he elaborated in his words and I did not reach the end of his view in all his words; but the matter is clear that we hold tevilah at its proper time is not a mitzvah, as all the poskim wrote. And therefore women do not immerse at their proper time when their husband is not in the city; and so widows and virgins who are niddot do not immerse at all at their proper time; and so too, even if she did not have relations, she does not immerse until after five and seven days, as above in the siman before this, seif 11. Nevertheless, she is permitted to immerse on Shabbat, as Terumas HaDeshen wrote siman 255 and Beit Yosef, and as is proven in the Gemara and poskim that there is no prohibition in the matter because it appears like miykar; and so it is implied below siman 199 seif 7. Only Terumas HaDeshen wrote that nowadays they practice to be stringent regarding a yoledet and the like, and he forced a reason, writing and these are his words: since the custom now is to be careful and prevent heating on Shabbat, even though there is no reason in the matter — nevertheless one cannot say that it appears like miykar, for they are careful about heating, and it is proven that their intent is to immerse, and it appears like fixing. And for this reason it appears that women practice to be careful not to immerse on Shabbat unless her husband is in the city and it was not possible for her beforehand, for in such a case it is a mitzvah because of onah end quote. It is implied from his words that when her husband is not in the city and he comes on Friday evening, even though it was possible beforehand — nevertheless, since she did not neglect in what she failed to immerse earlier, it is permitted to immerse on Shabbat. And so Beit Yosef ruled that it is permitted to immerse on Shabbat in every case — and this is the main halachah, as I explained. Therefore, in a place where there is no custom one should not be stringent, as the Rav wrote. And so Maharam Lublin wrote in responsum siman 53 and siman 71. See there. And regarding what he wrote about a widow, etc. — Maharam Lublin wrote in responsum siman 53: a virgin kalah whose immersion occurred on Friday night — it appears that one should permit, even though they practice not to have intercourse of mitzvah until after Shabbat, because other kinds of closeness are also mitzvah. And it is uncertain regarding a widow, for since it is forbidden to come upon her with first intercourse, they separate her from all other closeness — which is not so regarding a virgin. Nevertheless, it is good to precede her immersion before Shabbat. See there — he elaborated; and so they practice.`,
    '2#ד': `"And in a place where they practiced to be stringent, etc." It is uncertain what relation this stringency has to that. And one may say that this is the meaning: and in a place where they practiced to be stringent not to immerse on Shabbat except exactly at her proper time; and likewise in what he wrote, for the reason that one should not distance the immersion from the chafifah; and on Shabbat she cannot, because it is not at her proper time — one should not be lenient.`,
    '3#ד': `"And kalot, etc." It appears that this is specifically for us, where kalot do not immerse on the seventh day, as I wrote in siman 196 s.k. 20; and as is clear in Mahari"l there, who deals with such a case. But in a place where kalot immerse on the seventh day, or for us if her immersion occurred on the seventh day — for example, she had a mishap during her counting days and then immerses immediately after seven — she cannot immerse by day, for on this day (not only because of sarach batah alone) they are forbidden (but) because lest she see and annul everything retroactively, and be found a zavah retroactively, as stated in the Gemara and poskim, as above.`,
  },
  'torat-hashlamim': {
    '2#א': `So as not to nullify being fruitful and multiplying. But without this reason, tevilah at its proper time is not a mitzvah. And why is it forbidden to immerse on Shabbat and on Yom Kippur, even when the time has arrived, as is explained in Orach Chayyim siman 554 and siman 613? Since there is no mitzvah in the immersion itself, but only because of the mitzvah of onah — and on those days relations are forbidden — therefore, per this, also when the ninth of Av falls on Shabbat, as Rama ruled in the hagahah in Orach Chayyim siman 554, that they practice to forbid marital relations — if so, she is forbidden to immerse. And so regarding ola on Shabbat, in the name of a responsum of Rabbi Yitzchak of Bruna — and not like what Shelah wrote on page 101 side a, to permit because of immersion of mitzvah. And in truth this is not so, for there is no mitzvah at all in the immersion itself; examine. And so I heard in the name of the famous Gaon, Rabbi Shimon, may his memory be blessed, that he did not wish to permit in such a case.`,
    '2#ג': `After childbirth. And if she transgressed and immersed — Chut HaShani wrote siman 70: if unintentionally, she is permitted in every case; and if intentionally, she is forbidden for that Shabbat, and afterward she is permitted. And on Yom Tov she is permitted in every case, even intentionally. See there — his words at length. Now, certainly what he brings there as proof from a responsum of Rashba siman 485, brought in Beit Yosef in Tur Orach Chayyim siman 503 — that on Yom Tov we do not forbid at all if one transgressed and cooked — is no proof at all, for the words of Rashba are not agreed to by the majority of poskim, as is explained and proven from the words of the Rosh and other poskim regarding this inquiry of one who transgressed and baked, brought in Beit Yosef siman 527; and similarly Rambam in chapter 23 of Hilchot Shabbat, that one who raises terumot and maasrot on Yom Tov intentionally should not eat until Motzaei Yom Tov; and so regarding ola on Shabbat in siman 503. And so is the view of Mordechai and Beit Yosef in siman 503, who ruled to permit if he transgressed and cooked. One may say that he speaks of unintentional action; or even of intentional action on Shabbat, when he has nothing to eat — and because of kavod Shabbat, per the view of the Rosh and his allies, brought in Beit Yosef siman 527, and not because of Rashba's reason. Nevertheless, his words are correct lemaaseh, that even if she immersed on Shabbat intentionally she is permitted for Motzaei Shabbat, since this is only a general stringency; and even the concern of prohibition is only rabbinic, because it appears like fixing — we do not penalize even one who acted intentionally to forbid him forever, as Chut HaShani wrote there. And it is uncertain regarding cooking, that it is forbidden to him forever — for there it is a Torah prohibition; and further, here the person intends to fix, and we do not penalize so much, as stated in Gittin page 54. And even though in chapter 2 of Terumot, mishnah 3, it implies that maaser and cooking are equal regarding this, for we include them in one chapter — and regarding maaser too he intends to fix, like this woman — certainly this is not so, as Chut HaShani wrote there. And so it appears to me, as is proven from the words of Rambam: that in chapter 2 of Hilchot Shabbat, regarding cooking, he ruled like R. Yehudah, that intentionally it is forbidden to him forever, and unintentionally it is forbidden to him until Motzaei Shabbat; and in chapter 23 he ruled that one who tithes on Shabbat unintentionally may eat immediately, and intentionally only until Motzaei Shabbat. But certainly, as he wrote (and these words of Rambam escaped him, and to the author of Tosafot Yom Tov in his commentary on this mishnah — see there). And all the more so that this is only a general stringency and custom; and all the more on Yom Tov, where nothing at all was mentioned in the words of Terumat HaDeshen, the Rav, and other acharonim to forbid except on Shabbat — but on Yom Tov it appears that one should not be stringent at all, for do not add upon it (and see Orach Chayyim siman 501). And I did not know from where the author of Chut HaShani came to forbid on Yom Tov lechatchila, and he wrote there so in the name of Terumas HaDeshen — and there he did not mention Yom Tov at all. And also according to the reason that Terumas HaDeshen wrote, it appears to me that it is specifically Shabbat — examine. And see in responsum Maharam Lublin siman 53.`,
    '3#א': `Because of sarach batah. That she thinks her mother immersed on the night of the seventh, and she will do so too. But on day seven it is a complete prohibition, as the Tur wrote — lest she see and annul retroactively (and see Taz s.k. 8 in his explanation of the Tur's words — his words are somewhat astonishing; uncertain). And so it is implied and explained in the Gemara and all the poskim. However, I saw in Mahari"l at the beginning of the laws of Niddah that he wrote that even on day seven the reason is because of sarach batah; and he also wrote that for us, whose wives are not doubt zavot, etc. — and his words are astonishing, and it is uncertain; and perhaps an error fell into the books. And Shach wrote that one who immerses on day eight, where it is forbidden because of sarach batah alone, they practice to be lenient to immerse before dark, only that she comes home after it grows dark — unlike one who immerses on day seven, where one should not be lenient, but only immerse after dark; and one should protest against one who does not act so. And Shach wrote further that there is no distinction whether she has a daughter or not — and this is simple end quote.`,
    '3#ב': `And kalot. And Shach wrote: and specifically for us, where kalot do not immerse on the seventh day; but in a place where kalot immerse on the seventh day for their sight, or for us if her immersion occurred on the seventh day — for example, she had a mishap during her counting days and then immerses immediately after seven — she cannot immerse on day seven. It is not only because of sarach batah alone that they are forbidden, but because lest she see and annul everything retroactively, and be found a zavah retroactively, as stated in the Gemara and poskim end quote. And his words are astonishing at first glance, since the reason is that they do not come to the chassan until the night — if so, even if she annuls retroactively there is no concern of prohibition. And it is possible to say that Shach's intent is that nevertheless on this seventh day we decree, lest she come to the chassan by day — unlike day eight, where it is only a decree because of sarach batah; we do not decree so much, decree upon decree; examine.`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '2#ב': `And she is permitted to immerse on Friday night. In Tur he brought the view of Rashi Chananel, who holds that tevilah at its proper time is a mitzvah; and he brings proof from the Yerushalmi, which says: a woman who has a veset for her husband — her husband calculates the days of her veset and comes upon her; she said that it is forbidden for a woman to stand in her tumah end quote. And Me'iri wrote that there is no proof from this, for one may say that therefore it is forbidden for her to stand in her tumah, lest her husband come upon her. And it appears to me to answer that when we say "her husband calculates the days of her veset and comes upon her" — they did not say this specifically in a case where it is clear to the woman when her husband will come; rather, in every manner, even if she thought he would not come at that time. And if so, it is proven that there is a prohibition for a woman to stand in her tumah — not because of her husband, but because of tevilah at its proper time being a mitzvah. And upon this the husband relies and comes upon her — she certainly acted according to law. And per this it would be permitted to immerse on Shabbat; but Rashi Chananel holds that the halachah is that tevilah at its proper time is not a mitzvah from the immersion itself, but from the side of being fruitful and multiplying; and therefore Rama ruled specifically when her husband is in the city, as will be written shortly.`,
    '2#ג': `After childbirth, etc. In Terumas HaDeshen siman 255 they asked about a yoledet who immersed after eighty days for a female, or within that time — and the case was that she could have immersed seven or eight days earlier from the side of cleanliness, but of her own will she waits, because she does not yet have much strength — whether she may immerse on Shabbat, even though it is possible for her to immerse beforehand, or not. And he answered that Mordechai wrote that they do not permit immersing on Shabbat except because of tevilah at its proper time being a mitzvah; and this case was not at her proper time, since it was possible for her beforehand. And afterward he wrote: if it was not possible for her beforehand, for example because of oness — one may say that even according to Rashi, who ruled that tevilah at its proper time is not a mitzvah, nevertheless it is permitted to immerse because of the mitzvah of fulfilling onah; and even when it is not the time of onah it is considered a mitzvah, for a man gladdens his wife in relations, as stated in chapter Elu Devarim in Pesachim, etc. And he concluded there: women practice to be careful not to immerse on Shabbat unless her husband is in the city and it was not possible for her beforehand, for in such a case it was an immersion of mitzvah because of onah. And even though in chapter 2 of Beitzah we say that every tamei person immerses on Shabbat because it appears like miykar — nowadays is different, for the practice is not to heat oneself on Shabbat in any water; if so, it does not appear like miykar end quote — his words did not permit except when she had no obligation to immerse before Shabbat, for example because of oness, and her husband was with her on Friday evening; and he does not reduce this except when he is not in the city at the time of her immersion. But it is simple that this does not help if he did not come until Friday evening and she had an opportunity to immerse beforehand, for it does not help her that her husband was not in the city on a weekday — nevertheless she should have immersed on a weekday. And in Agur and Mahari"v they brought Darkei Moshe, and Me'iri was more stringent — that even if she was forced and could not immerse before Shabbat, and her husband comes on Friday evening, she should not immerse on Shabbat; and their proof is from Mordechai mentioned above. And Beit Yosef disagrees with them and concludes to be lenient, even when it was possible beforehand, because of the reason that it appears like miykar; and the words of Terumas HaDeshen above he did not bring at all. And Darkei Moshe wrote: since they practiced to immerse on Shabbat, this implies that we do not hold like Mordechai — if so, it should be permitted even if she could have immersed beforehand; nevertheless it appears to me to reconcile the custom and to say that whoever could not immerse beforehand — even by a stringency that women themselves practiced — is called tevilah at its proper time, and she immerses on Shabbat; but in a place where it was possible, no. And nevertheless I do not find to be stringent in this in a place where they did not practice to be stringent end quote. And so he ruled here in Shulchan Aruch that the matter depends on custom. And his words are astonishing, since according to Mordechai, and Agur in the name of the great ones, and Mahari"v, and Mahari"l — they forbid immersing on Shabbat entirely; and Terumas HaDeshen permits only when it was not possible at first, but when it was possible it is forbidden, even if her husband comes on Friday evening. Why did Rama be lenient here in a place where there is no custom, if her husband comes on Friday evening, even if it was possible — and similarly after childbirth — and he is against all these great ones? Also Me'iri disagrees with Rama on this and is stringent in every place; only that he wrote that according to the words of Terumas HaDeshen above it is implied that specifically when her husband is in the city and it was possible beforehand she is forbidden on Shabbat; but when her husband is not in the city and he comes on Friday evening, even though it was possible beforehand, she is permitted on Shabbat — because women do not immerse without their husband being in the city. And this is not with precision; certainly Terumas HaDeshen did not intend to permit this, but his intent is that we require her husband to be in the city at the time of her immersion; and there is no distinction whether he came on Friday evening or earlier — nevertheless we require that it be in a situation where it was not possible at first to immerse. With this we should rule according to the view of Terumas HaDeshen, whose words are intermediate between those who are completely stringent and those who are completely lenient — namely, that whoever can immerse before Shabbat without any oness and did not immerse is forbidden to immerse on Shabbat, even if her husband came on Friday evening; and whoever could not because of some oness, like that case of Terumas HaDeshen that we mentioned, should be permitted to immerse on Shabbat in every place.`,
    '2#ה': `That it is forbidden to come upon her with first intercourse on Shabbat. Because he acquires her through this intercourse, as stated in Orach Chayyim siman 39 and Even HaEzer siman 63 — therefore one should not make a wedding for a widow on Shabbat when her immersion falls on Friday night. And if she immersed before Shabbat, he must come upon her specifically on Friday evening after the chuppah; yichud in one room does not help to have intercourse with her on Shabbat for the first time. So too in Mordechai, chapter Mishpatim, in the name of Rabbi Meir; and so too in Mahari"l, laws of Shabbat, in the name of Maharash, teacher of Mahari"l — we require complete intercourse specifically. Even though in Sefer Masat Binyamin siman 90 it is written that yichud also acquires in a place fit for intercourse — nevertheless, since great ones forbid, one should be stringent; and it will be explained further in Even HaEzer siman 63.`,
    '3#_': `She is forbidden to immerse by day, etc. Explanation: the law of the Torah is that a niddah who immerses on her seventh day — even if there were no clean days — immerses specifically at night; regarding her, it depends specifically on days. But a zavah who counts seven clean days in her counting — we say "part of the day is like the whole day," since she counted part of the seventh day in cleanliness, she immerses even by day; and we derive this from the verse, "and afterward she shall be pure" — after the act she shall be pure. Only the Sages forbade immersing by day, lest she have relations while it is still day, and afterward on that same day see and annul retroactively — and it be found that he came upon her in prohibition. Therefore the Tur wrote: our women, even though they are doubt zavot — meaning that they count seven clean days — and if so there would always be at least eight days with the day of sight, and she would have permission to immerse by day, since the seven days have already been completed — nevertheless she should not immerse by day. And with this the words of Shulchan Aruch here are explained; and the reason is because of sarach batah — meaning, her daughter will see that her mother immersed on day eight, and will think that on day seven she immersed, and she too will do so.`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `Question: A kalah whose day of chuppah is the seventh day of her sight — may she immerse by day, so that the chuppah not be a chuppah of niddah, for one should not make a chuppah of niddah lechatchila, as is explained in Even HaEzer siman 61 — for so is the view of Rambam; and Shach in siman 196 wrote that even though we are not meticulous about this, as is explained in Even HaEzer there — nevertheless, certainly where possible in greater purity is preferable. See there. Answer: Behold, in Shulchan Aruch siman 197 Rama wrote in the name of Mahari"l that kalot who immerse before the chuppah may immerse by day, for they do not come to the chassan until the night. And Shach there wrote: it appears that this is specifically for us, where kalot do not immerse on the seventh day — as is clear in Mahari"l, who deals with such a case. But in a place where kalot immerse on the seventh day, or for us if she had a mishap during her counting days and then immerses immediately after seven — she cannot immerse by day, for on day seven it is forbidden not only because of sarach batah alone, but lest she see and annul and be found a zavah retroactively end quote. And in Minchat Yaakov he was astonished at this, for according to the reason that they do not come to the chassan until the night, there is no concern lest she annul — for then she will truly not have relations. And he wrote to reconcile it with difficulty. And similarly Ba'er Heitev challenged Shach; and in responsum Pischei Mevinim volume 2 he also attacks Shach on this and rules to be lenient. And so in responsum Even HaGra siman 20 he wrote on Shach that he erred in what he saw, for in Hagahot S.D. it is written explicitly in the name of Mahari"l that there is no concern for the view of Rashi, nor for sarach batah. And so in Sefer Seder Taharah; and the Gaon Maharaz, may his memory be blessed, elaborated greatly on this and concluded at the end: the whole matter is heard that if the seven clean days are completed on the day of the chuppah itself, she should not immerse by day, since she will be with the chassan that night — there is in the matter concern of sarach batah; and even if it is day eight, it appears that sarach batah also applies, though one may distinguish, as Maaseh Yom wrote on the words of Shach, that on day eight one may be more lenient, for it is like a decree upon a decree. Nevertheless, it is not compelled that Shach said so, because he relied on Rama's reason that they do not come to the chassan until the night — which he wrote according to his version; and according to what is written, this is not so. And also, in any case the chuppah must be at night, as implied by Rama's wording; and it is not reasonable to be lenient when she goes to her house after the chuppah and does not come until the night. But per my humble view, even in such a case, where the chuppah will be at night, one should not be lenient — for I did not find that Mahari"l is lenient regarding immersion on the day of the chuppah, except when the chassan will not come upon her on that night at all — which is the night before the chuppah. But if the chuppah will be in the evening, it does not appear to be lenient for this reason, for this is not found like Mahari"l; and all the more on day seven one should not be lenient, even if the chuppah will be at night. And in Sefer Degul MeRevavah he also wrote to challenge Shach, that on day seven too one should permit, for the concern of "she will see and annul" does not apply. And he wrote that nevertheless the heart of Minchat Yaakov was not filled to disagree with Shach, even though he sensed this attack. Nevertheless, in a pressing situation it appears to be lenient even on day seven — but only on condition that they not establish the chuppah until the stars actually emerge. But to establish the chuppah by day and rely on the fact that they do not seclude them until the night does not help, per their understanding — that Mahari"l is lenient even though intercourse will be at night, since they do not come until the night. And they explain that what is meant by "they do not come until the night" is because the chuppah is at night; or he speaks of his own reasoning, saying that on day seven, where the concern is because of zavah intercourse, there is greater reason to be concerned, and one should not rely on not secluding them until the night — for this only helps on day eight, where the concern is only because of sarach batah. But for day seven it does not help, except that she needs to wait after the chuppah until the night. And according to what is written, Mahari"l does not be lenient at all regarding immersion on the day of the chuppah. If so, one may say that if she did not immerse on the day before the chuppah, she again needs to immerse at night, whether the chuppah will be by day or at night. And according to the custom of these countries, where on the night before the chuppah they make a feast in the kalah's house, which they call the chassan's meal, and they also bring the chassan there with the youths — after the feast they seclude the chassan and kalah in a special room. Nevertheless, it appears that she may immerse properly by day, for sarach batah is not relevant here — for even though they seclude them, nevertheless it is known that the yichud is not for intercourse, but only in order to fulfill the words of Chazal: do not sanctify a woman until he sees her, and also so that his heart be bold toward her. And in such a case there is no concern of sarach batah, as in Hagahot S.D. that I brought above. But on the day of the chuppah it appears that one should not be lenient. But per my humble view, in a place where women are insistent about this and are ashamed that the kalah is not pure — because the matter is publicized at the time of giving kiddushin and when they return from the chuppah, and their joy becomes grief for them — one should permit her to immerse on the day before the chuppah, since otherwise it is fitting to act in purity, as Shach wrote in siman 193 that so is preferable; and so Beit Shmuel. But she needs to immerse again at night, since there are several great ones who hold that even if she immersed on day seven, bediavad her immersion did not count and she needs to return and immerse at night — as Shach wrote there, and so is the view of Mahari"l. If so, one should not be lenient in this immersion. And so in responsum Chakham Tzvi it is explained, that he wrote regarding one who permitted immersing on Friday evening voluntarily on the seventh day — he did not act well and transgressed a rabbinic prohibition; and for some of the great ones, even bediavad her immersion did not count, etc. And he concluded that one should not permit except that they immerse in cold water on Friday night, or by cooling their heat, etc. See there. And so I ruled halachah lemaaseh. But where the day of the chuppah is day eight, and the hour requires that she immerse on the day before the chuppah — then she does not need to return and immerse again at night, for on day eight all agree that bediavad her immersion counted. However, one should be stringent not to make the chuppah until the night; and in particular according to the custom of these countries, where immediately when they return from the chuppah they seclude them alone in a room — sarach batah applies here. Therefore one should make the chuppah only at night. And if because of some reason and necessity it must be made by day — in any case one should not seclude them after the chuppah until the night. Per my humble view I wrote the words of Ze'ira Kodesh Efrayim Zalman Margoliyot.`,
  },
};
